A||||6||VISCERAL||Suspension bushing removal
Z||000000||VISCERAL||05-17-99||08:47 AM||||How does one remove the rusted on sleeves and bushings on the cradle and arms without garage help, just home tools? Thanks, Patrick
Z||000001||VISCERAL||05-17-99||08:58 AM||||Do the metal sleeves stay in the arms and you simply gut out the old rubber bushings then push in the prothane poly bushings since the prothanes came without metal sleeves. This would be a lot simpler to do than to press out the metal sleeves. <br>BTW are there eight balljoints to replace on the car, right? Thanks, Patrick
Z||000002||theogre||05-17-99||03:03 PM||||there are 6 ball joints plus 4 tie rod ends.<p>read the notes at  <A HREF="http://www.prothane.com" TARGET=_blank>www.prothane.com</A>  and  <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>www.suspension.com</A> <p>current product calls for the shells to stay in.
Z||000003||Patrick||05-17-99||03:27 PM||||<br>Ummm.... you're welcome, Visceral.<p>What did I do?    <IMG SRC="http://forums.gagames.com/forums/biggrin.gif"> <p>
Z||000004||RICK||05-17-99||04:56 PM||||I just replaced the front bushing's on my 87, some of the bushings pushed out by hand (real bad shape)others I had to use a press to get them out.  Even with the press I still had to go in and clean out some of the rubber that was stuck to the insides
Z||000005||PaUL||05-17-99||09:28 PM||||I read somewhere that you should'nt use a press to remove the old rubber bushings because the pressure applied could expand the metal shell. The rubber bulges on the sides when pressed. Then when you put the new poly bushings in, they would be too loose and cause squeaking. You should use a torch to burn the old rubber out.
Z||000006||RICK||05-19-99||08:24 AM||||I didnt have any problem with the metal expanding.  When I put the poly bushing's back in they had to be pressed in.  I tried to burn out one and it worked but it was already loose.  BTW it is a big mess to burn those things out.  <br>Good Luck!
